---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **발판이 되나.** (`/foothold`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **순위표를 안 만든다.** 「어느 회사가 1등인가」를 안 낸다. 발판이 있느냐 없느냐만 본다.
 * ⛔ **교란 셋을 죽인 것을 화면에 적는다** — 회사 크기 · 때 · 나라.
 *    ⭐ 그중 「때」가 제일 셌다. 발판 뒤 작품은 정의상 나중 작품이라 그렇다.
 * ⛔ **뒤집힌 다섯 곳을 숨기지 않는다.** 영어권 넷에서 발판이 안 통한다.
 * ⛔ 왜 그런지는 이 자료에 **없다.**
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-foothold.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-foothold.json';

const w = data.withinTitle;
const TITLE = 'A company that has charted in a country once charts there again';
const DESC =
  `Across ${data.firmsCompared} Korean production companies and ${data.marketCount} Netflix markets, a series `
  + `charts in ${w.withFootholdPc}% of the countries where its company had already charted, against `
  + `${w.withoutFootholdPc}% where it had not — ${w.liftTimes}× — measured inside single titles so that age, `
  + `title strength and company size cannot explain it.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};

const 뒤집힌 = data.byMarket.filter((m) => m.withFootholdPc <= m.withoutFootholdPc);
const 안뒤집힌 = data.byMarket.filter((m) => m.withFootholdPc > m.withoutFootholdPc);
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/foothold">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Every other page here counts titles. This one counts the companies behind them, and asks the
    question a company actually has: does getting into a country once make the next one easier?
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>The raw answer, and why you should not believe it yet</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>How this is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>A company's next series</th><th>Charted</th><th>Chances</th><th>Rate</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">In countries where that company had already charted</td>
            <td>{data.withFoothold.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td>{data.withFootholdChances.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.withFootholdPc}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">In countries where it had not</td>
            <td>{data.withoutFoothold.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td>{data.withoutFootholdChances.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.withoutFootholdPc}%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="warn">
      That is {data.liftPoints} points — {data.liftTimes}× — and on its own it is not worth much. <b>A series with a foothold behind
      it is, by construction, a later series.</b> Netflix's country charts grew over the
      {' '}{data.weeksSpanned} weeks measured here, and Korean titles grew with them. The gap could be
      the foothold, or it could be nothing more than the calendar.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Killing the calendar: each series compared against itself</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">{w.how}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Inside one series, on its own weeks</th><th>Charted</th><th>Chances</th><th>Rate</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">Countries where its company had already charted</td>
            <td>{w.withFoothold.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td>{w.withFootholdChances.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{w.withFootholdPc}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Countries where it had not</td>
            <td>{w.withoutFoothold.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td>{w.withoutFootholdChances.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{w.withoutFootholdPc}%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>It survives.</b> {w.titles} series could be measured this way, and the gap is
      {' '}{w.liftPoints} points — {w.liftTimes}×, slightly <em>wider</em> than the raw figure rather
      than narrower. Whatever is happening is not the calendar, is not that one title was stronger
      than another, and is not that one company is bigger than another: the comparison never leaves a
      single title on a single set of weeks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>It is not the big companies carrying it</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Company size (series that charted)</th><th>Companies</th>
            <th>With a foothold</th><th>Without</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.byFirmSize.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{b.band}</td>
              <td>{b.firms}</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.withFootholdPc}%</td>
              <td>{b.withoutFootholdPc}%</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Read the last two columns across every row: the gap is there in all four bands. The
      {' '}{data.byFirmSize[data.byFirmSize.length - 1].band} band has the <em>lowest</em> rates on both
      sides — a company with a large catalogue has many series that chart in few places, which drags
      both columns down together without closing the gap between them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where it does not hold — {data.marketsWhereItReverses} markets of {data.marketsMeasured}</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">
      Splitting by country removes the last obvious objection: that a company's foothold countries are
      simply the countries that take Korean titles easily. Inside a single country that difference is
      gone. Of the {data.marketsMeasured} markets with enough chances on both sides to measure,
      {' '}<b>{안뒤집힌.length} point the same way</b> and {data.marketsWhereItReverses} do not —
      {' '}{data.marketsWhereItReversesNames.join(', ')}.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Market</th><th>With a foothold</th><th>Without</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {뒤집힌.map((m) => (
            <tr class="edge">
              <td class="nm">{m.iso2}</td>
              <td class="tot">{m.withFootholdPc}%</td>
              <td>{m.withoutFootholdPc}%</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="warn">
      <b>Alphabetical, not ranked.</b> Four of the {data.marketsWhereItReverses} are English-speaking markets. We are
      not going to tell you why, because this data does not say — but a company planning on the
      strength of an earlier hit should know that the pattern it is planning on is the one place it
      has not been observed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How much of the catalogue this covers</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Companies with two or more series that charted</td><td class="tot">{data.firmsCompared}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Companies dropped — only one series ever charted</td><td class="tot">{data.firmsDroppedSingleWork}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Markets</td><td class="tot">{data.marketCount}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Weeks</td><td class="tot">{data.weeksSpanned}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Chart rows read</td><td class="tot">{data.rowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>{data.firmsDroppedSingleWork} companies are not in any figure on this page.</b> A company with
      one charting series has no "next" series, so the question cannot be asked of it. That is a real
      limit, not a rounding note: it means everything here describes companies that have already
      charted twice.
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/foothold" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix and Wikidata</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li><b>Why this cannot say why.</b> {data.cannotAnswer}</li>
      <li><b>Series only.</b> {data.whySeriesOnly}</li>
      <li><b>Inside one company, never between two.</b> {data.whyWithinFirm}</li>
      <li>
        <b>Russia is excluded</b> throughout this site, because Netflix withdrew from it and the
        remaining weeks are not comparable with the others.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/who-makes-it">How many companies fill half the shelf</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/reach">How far titles travel</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
